<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- pinion R1796 §5.4 §3 — an act the HOST performs, and the order of what it
     answers with.

     WHAT THIS IS FOR. W3C SCXML 6.2.5 makes a `<send>` `type` an extensible
     identifier: the set of Event I/O Processors is open, and a platform may
     serve its own. SCE emits a DISPATCH for a type the build declared and a
     refusal (`error.execution`) for one it did not, so whether this door is
     open at all is decided at compile time.

     Nothing in pinion had ever declared one. The door existed at the previous
     pin and was unused; what this pin brought is that a handler now answers
     with a LIST of events IN ORDER rather than an optional single one — and an
     ordering guarantee that nothing exercises is an ordering guarantee nobody
     has checked.

     ⚠ A FIXTURE, NOT A WIDGET, and deliberately outside `widgets/` for the
     reason `self_answering_raise.scxml` is: every chart in that directory is a
     product surface with a binding. This one exists to make an engine
     guarantee observable from here.

     ⚠ WHAT IT IS *NOT* DOING. It does not open a third escape hatch. §3 says
     `Effect(opaque)` and `External(opaque)` are the only two, and a host-served
     `<send>` whose handler an APPLICATION registers would be a third. The type
     named here is the framework's own and is served by framework code; letting
     an application register one is a §3 question and needs a spec round, which
     `pinion_core::host_served`'s module doc records rather than quietly
     answers.

     THE SHAPE. `asking` sends to the host and waits. The host's handler
     answers with TWO events, and the chart can only reach `settled` by seeing
     them in the order the host wrote: `first` moves it to `heard_first`, and
     only from there does `second` reach `settled`. A machine that saw them in
     the other order — or saw only one — stops somewhere else and says so by
     the state it is in. That is the whole point: the order is not asserted
     about a queue, it is asserted about where the document ended up.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       datamodel="null"
       initial="idle">

  <state id="idle">
    <transition event="go" target="asking"/>
  </state>

  <state id="asking">
    <!-- The act. `type` names a processor the BUILD declared, so codegen emits
         a dispatch here; without the declaration this same element compiles to
         an `error.execution` raise and the chart would never leave `asking`. -->
    <onentry>
      <send type="pinion.fixture.host" event="ask"/>
    </onentry>
    <!-- Only `first` is accepted here. A host that answered `second` first
         would leave the machine sitting in `asking`, which is what makes the
         order observable rather than assumed. -->
    <transition event="first" target="heard_first"/>
  </state>

  <state id="heard_first">
    <transition event="second" target="settled"/>
  </state>

  <state id="settled"/>

</scxml>
